WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.870
-Our first guest tonight is the
Academy Award-winning filmmaker

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.271
of documentaries like
"Roger and Me,"

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:06.973
"Bowling for Columbine,"
and "Fahrenheit 9/11."

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.842
He's the executive producer
of "Planet of the Humans,"

00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:11.510
which is available on YouTube.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.446
His podcast,
"Rumble with Michael Moore,"

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:15.447
is available
on all podcast platforms.

00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:18.051
Please welcome back to the show
Michael Moore.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:21.854
Welcome back, Michael.
It's always so good to see you.

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:25.425
-Thanks for having me here
in your home, sort of.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:27.626
-Sort of. And the same with you.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:30.530
So, you've fought
for societal change

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.732
pretty much your whole career,
your whole life.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:34.768
How do you feel
about this moment right now

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.103
when we're seeing people from
sort of all walks of lives,

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.907
all diverse backgrounds,
basically risking their lives

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:43.109
being on the street
to fight for this moment?

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:48.280
-I am so uplifted
by the response.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.417
And you're right --
they are risking --

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.785
Everybody who goes out there
and marches in demonstrations

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:55.488
is risking their life for this.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.791
When history looks back on this,
when they see

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:00.059
that literally
millions around the world

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:06.065
took to the streets
during a pandemic, it's, um --

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.367
it gives me a lot of hope,
actually.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:12.338
I'm in a hopeful mood right now

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:16.843
in terms of what we're going --

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:19.012
First of all,
just with what's happened

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.980
in the last couple of weeks.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.450
That, um --

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.755
That, uh, white citizens

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.157
didn't leave it up
to black Americans

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:34.227
to have to go
and fight this alone.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:38.131
And that these demonstrations,

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.299
black, Hispanic, white,

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:42.669
Asian Americans,
Native Americans --

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:46.105
I mean, all men, women,
old, young.

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:49.842
It's the country I want
to live in, if I can say that.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.310
I, um...

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:57.016
I believe very strongly
that we have to change this.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.285
I believe
in defunding the police

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.687
and explaining
exactly what that means.

00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:04.190
Obviously, nobody wants
to get rid of the police

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.759
in the sense of our safety.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:10.196
But the way we've been doing it
is wrong.

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:12.265
It's wrong for the police.

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.801
You know, there was an officer

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.236
on one of the cable news shows
this week

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:19.572
saying 90% of their calls...
-Yeah.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:22.475
-...are for things
they're not trained to do.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.078
Why not when we call 911 --

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:27.079
You know, it's not
just a police 911.

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:29.215
You call, and you say,
"My house is on fire,"

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.849
they send the fire department.

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:34.754
You call and you say, "My
husband's had a heart attack,"

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:36.990
they send the EMT.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.659
Why not
when they call for something

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.260
that's a mental-health issue,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.797
something that's a drug
or alcohol addiction issue,

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.433
something that --

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.802
I mean, you need
the police obviously

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:55.074
for homicide, rape, robbery,
men abusing women --

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.209
you know, dangerous situations.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:00.813
And the ones who are trained
to do that are trained well,

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:03.049
go through a review process

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.284
to make sure that
we're not hiring any racists.

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:08.488
They should be embraced

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:12.791
and paid well
for taking that risk for us.

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:16.996
Um, you know, I mean, I just --

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:20.900
I can't stress enough that this
is our moment to fix this.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.438
We don't have to see another
person say, "I can't breathe."

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:28.541
We just --
We can't let that happen again.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.376
We all have to commit to that.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:33.946
-You mentioned how we don't
want to see anyone ever say

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:35.447
"I can't breathe" again.

00:03:35.447 --> 00:03:36.915
And, of course, this was a case

00:03:36.915 --> 00:03:40.386
where we only saw it
because someone filmed it.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.088
You, obviously,
as a documentarian

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:44.090
try to change people's minds.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.193
Yet, you, I think, would admit

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:51.264
and have said that
the young girl who filmed this

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:53.633
made the documentary
of the year.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:59.405
-Yes, I said that
on my podcast last week, that --

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.809
Her name is Darnella Frazier.

00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:07.713
And she made the most important
nonfiction film maybe in years.

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:09.815
This brave young girl --

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:12.251
she's a teenager,
she's a high-school student --

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:15.754
stood there, stood there
with the cops with guns

00:04:15.754 --> 00:04:17.523
right there with her.

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:19.425
Anything could have happened.

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:23.896
And then all of a sudden,
um, you know, she --

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:27.533
Whatever in her
gave her that bravery,

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:30.202
that cool, calm, collected --

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:33.939
She shot that
for almost nine minutes.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:37.409
No experience. No, you know --

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:41.914
Just completely -- It was --
She should be honored.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:47.219
I said this on my show
that at the Oscars next year,

00:04:47.219 --> 00:04:50.923
we should acknowledge the most
important nonfiction film

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.591
was not by a filmmaker.

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:55.161
But my point is, is that
I want all young people --

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:56.663
People are watching this now.

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:59.197
You are filmmakers now,
thanks to --

00:04:59.197 --> 00:05:02.802
Steve Jobs had the idea
to put a camera in a phone.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:05.171
I would have loved to have been
there in that brainstorming

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:08.607
session where, you know,
Steve says,

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:10.676
"Let's put something else
in this phone."

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:13.413
And then they're
at the whiteboard.

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.848
Somebody says, "How about a
rotisserie set?" "No, no, no."

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:17.250
"Maybe just
a fork, spoon, and knife."

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.920
"No, no, no, no, no.
Uh, how about a camera?!"

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.892
That idea right there may
end up saving so many lives.

